Certainly! Here's an example of natural selection using MATLAB code:
% Set up initial population
pop_size = 100; % number of individuals in population
trait_values = rand(pop_size,1); % random trait values for each individual
fitness = trait_values.^2; % fitness is proportional to trait value squared
% Simulate natural selection over multiple generations
num_generations = 10;
for i = 1:num_generations
    % Calculate probability of survival for each individual
    survival_prob = fitness / sum(fitness);

    % Sample new population based on survival probabilities
    new_pop = randsample(pop_size, pop_size, true, survival_prob);

    % Mutate trait values of new population
    mutation_rate = 0.01;
    new_trait_values = trait_values(new_pop) + mutation_rate * randn(pop_size,1);

    % Calculate fitness of new population
    new_fitness = new_trait_values.^2;

    % Replace old population with new population
    trait_values = new_trait_values;
    fitness = new_fitness;
end
% Plot final distribution of trait values
histogram(trait_values); xlabel('Trait value'); ylabel('Frequency');
In this example, we start with a population of 100 individuals with random trait values. Fitness is proportional to the square of the trait value. We then simulate natural selection over 10 generations by calculating the probability of survival for each individual based on their fitness, sampling a new population based on these probabilities, and mutating the trait values of the new population. Finally, we plot the distribution of trait values in the final population. This code demonstrates how natural selection can lead to the evolution of a trait over multiple generations.

