To calculate the horizontal and vertical components of a projectile's displacement at a given time t, you can use the following equations:
Horizontal displacement = (initial velocity x cos(theta)) x t
Vertical displacement = (initial velocity x sin(theta)) x t - (0.5 x g x t^2)
where:
- initial velocity is the initial speed of the projectile
- theta is the angle at which the projectile is launched
- g is the acceleration due to gravity (9.81 m/s^2)
To calculate the projectile's maximum height and range under the given conditions, you can use numerical methods such as the Euler method or the Runge-Kutta method. Here's an example Java code that uses the Euler method:
```
double initialVelocity = 20; // m/s
double theta = Math.PI / 4; // radians
double mass = 0.1; // kg
double dragCoefficient = 0.1; // kg/m
double windSpeed = 5; // m/s
double windDirection = Math.PI / 6; // radians
double timeStep = 0.01; // s
double totalTime = 10; // s
double x = 0;
double y = 0;
double vx = initialVelocity * Math.cos(theta);
double vy = initialVelocity * Math.sin(theta);
double ax = 0;
double ay = -g;
double v = initialVelocity;
double t = 0;
while (t <= totalTime) {
    double vwindx = windSpeed * Math.cos(windDirection);
    double vwindy = windSpeed * Math.sin(windDirection);
    double vrelx = vx - vwindx;
    double vrely = vy - vwindy;
    double vrel = Math.sqrt(vrelx * vrelx + vrely * vrely);
    double fdx = -0.5 * dragCoefficient * vrelx * vrel;
    double fdy = -0.5 * dragCoefficient * vrely * vrely;
    double fax = fdx / mass;
    double fay = fdy / mass;
    ax = fax;
    ay = fay - g;
    vx += ax * timeStep;
    vy += ay * timeStep;
    x += vx * timeStep;
    y += vy * timeStep;
    t += timeStep;
    if (y < 0) {
        break;
    }
}
double range = x;
double maxHeight = y;
System.out.println("Range: " + range + " m");
System.out.println("Max height: " + maxHeight + " m");
```
In this code, we first define the initial conditions of the projectile (initial velocity, launch angle, mass, drag coefficient, wind speed and direction, time step, and total time). Then, we use the Euler method to numerically solve the equations of motion for the projectile, taking into account the effects of air resistance and wind. The code calculates the horizontal and vertical displacements of the projectile at each time step, and stops the simulation when the projectile hits the ground (y < 0). Finally, the code outputs the range and maximum height of the projectile.
To incorporate the wind variables into the calculations, we first calculate the relative velocity of the projectile with respect to the wind (vrelx and vrely). Then, we calculate the drag force (fdx and fdy) using the relative velocity and the drag coefficient. We divide the drag force by the mass of the projectile to obtain the acceleration due to air resistance (fax and fay). Finally, we add the acceleration due to air resistance to the acceleration due to gravity to obtain the total acceleration of the projectile (ax and ay). We update the velocity and position of the projectile using the total acceleration, and repeat the process for each time step.

