There are several ways to perform sentiment analysis in Java, including using libraries such as Stanford CoreNLP, OpenNLP, and Apache OpenNLP. Here's an example Java code that uses the Stanford CoreNLP library to classify the sentiment of a given text:
```java
import java.util.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.sentiment.*;
public class SentimentAnalysis {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new AnnotationPipeline with sentiment analysis annotator
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, parse, sentiment");
        StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
        // Create an Annotation object with the input text
        Annotation annotation = new Annotation("I love this product!");
        // Run all the annotators on this text
        pipeline.annotate(annotation);
        // Get the sentiment score for the text
        List<CoreMap> sentences = annotation.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
        for (CoreMap sentence : sentences) {
            String sentiment = sentence.get(SentimentCoreAnnotations.SentimentClass.class);
            System.out.println("Sentiment: " + sentiment);
        }
    }
}
```
