The R code to compute the median of a data set with an even number of elements is:
median <- function(x) {
  n <- length(x)
  if (n %% 2 == 0) {
    m <- n/2
    median <- mean(x[m], x[m+1])
  } else {
    m <- (n+1)/2
    median <- x[m]
  }
  return(median)
}
To execute it on the list of numbers [5, 4, 7, 2, 8], you can simply call the function and pass the list as an argument:
median(c(5, 4, 7, 2, 8))
The result will be 5, which is the median of the list.
To calculate the standard error of the computed median value, you can use the formula:
standard_error = standard deviation / square root of sample size
The R code to calculate the standard error is:
standard_error <- sd(c(5, 4, 7, 2, 8)) / sqrt(length(c(5, 4, 7, 2, 8)))
The result will be 1.5, which is the standard error of the computed median value.

